% ============================================================================
% verb-type — three named verb types and what each actually is
% (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Verbs fall into several overlapping categories: an action verb describes
% a physical action or activity that can be seen or heard, a linking verb
% connects the subject to other words in the sentence, and an auxiliary
% ("helping") verb changes another verb's tense, voice, or mood. Recall is
% a binding query:
%
%     ? verb_type(action_verb, $D)
%       % physical_action_or_activity_that_can_be_seen_or_heard
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `verb_type(type, description)` carrying the source citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns
% the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a verb type that is not one of these three. The query also
% runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the verb type it names:
%
%     ? verb_type($T, changes_another_verbs_tense_voice_or_mood)
%       % auxiliary_verb
%
% The three verb types, as a little truth table:
%
%     type              description                                             quote (verbatim)
%     ----------------  --------------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     action_verb        physical_action_or_activity_that_can_be_seen_or_heard    "Most verbs describe a physical action or activity, something external that can be seen or heard."
%     linking_verb        connects_the_subject_to_other_words_in_the_sentence      "A linking verb is any verb, dynamic or stative, that directly connects or \"links\" the sentence's subject to other words in the sentence."
%     auxiliary_verb       changes_another_verbs_tense_voice_or_mood                "Auxiliary verbs, or \"helping verbs,\" are used in English to change another verb's tense, voice, or mood."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "Verbs: Definition and
% Examples" article, which also gives clean single-sentence definitions
% for stative verbs, modal auxiliary verbs, and phrasal verbs. WebFetch-
% verified before writing (twice). `trust consensus` — Grammarly is a
% reputable, widely-cited grammar-content company, the same tier this
% stdlib already reserves for other non-.gov language sources and the same
% source family already cited by `sentence-type.adj`/`part-of-speech.adj`/
% `noun-type.adj`. Picked using the mandatory full-tree-grep-before-
% scoping discipline — `grep -rilE "\bverb_type\b|\baction_verb\b|
% \blinking_verb\b|\bauxiliary_verb\b|\bhelping_verb\b|\btransitive_verb\b"
% code/specs/data/adj-facts-stdlib/` found ZERO hits, confirming a
% completely fresh topic before this file was written. A verb-related
% concept that is NOT one of these three — "transitive_verb", a real verb
% category the same page also covers, but only through worked examples
% and category description rather than a single formal definition
% sentence the way it does for the three tabled here — is deliberately
% NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a
% definition. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table verb_type {
    columns type, description

    row (action_verb, physical_action_or_activity_that_can_be_seen_or_heard)
    row (linking_verb, connects_the_subject_to_other_words_in_the_sentence)
    row (auxiliary_verb, changes_another_verbs_tense_voice_or_mood)

    source "Most verbs describe a physical action or activity, something external that can be seen or heard."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/parts-of-speech/verbs/"
    trust consensus
}
